    "evidence": "Challenge to the claim that \"the 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items\". The claim is arithmetically false on the invoice as submitted.\n\nLine-item arithmetic from the invoice:\n- Line 1, Pallet wrap: 3 x 120 = 360\n- Line 2, Corner boards: 2 x 45 = 90\n- Sum of line items: 360 + 90 = 450\n\nThe invoice header states a total due of 520. The invoice also states that no taxes, fees, or other lines appear on it, so 450 is the complete sum of the line items. 520 - 450 = 70, so the stated total exceeds the sum of the line items by 70.\n\nThe claim asserts equality between 520 and the sum of the line items. Equality fails: 520 != 450. The claim is therefore not supported by the submitted invoice; the invoice supports the opposite proposition, that the stated total does not equal the sum of its line items.\n\nScope of this evidence, stated honestly: it establishes only that the invoice is internally inconsistent by 70 units, not the cause. A missing line item, a pricing error, or a header error would each explain it, and the invoice alone does not identify which. Nothing in this evidence bears on whether the amounts are otherwise correct or collectible, and I make no claim about that. The single point at issue is the asserted equality, and on the face of the document that equality does not hold.",
