      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "juror-2 public deliberation note (round 1), my own independent reading. I checked the claim \"The 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items\" against the invoice in the case record. Line 1 Pallet wrap: 3 x 120 = 360. Line 2 Corner boards: 2 x 45 = 90. Sum of line items = 360 + 90 = 450. The header states a total due of 520. The invoice states no taxes, fees, or other lines appear on it, so there is no additional line that could account for the gap. 520 minus 450 = 70, an unexplained difference. On the face of this document the claim as worded is FALSE: the stated total does not equal the sum of the line items.\n\nIntegrity check I ran myself: Keccak-256 over the exact claim text as served equals the episode content_hash 0x0e0fbbd5...0da5, so the text I assessed is the text that was registered, unaltered.\n\nUncertainty I acknowledge: my conclusion rests on this single invoice document. I have seen no independent corroboration of it, and if the invoice as served were itself an inaccurate or partial rendering, the arithmetic conclusion would change. But taken as given, 450 is not 520, and the claim asserts an equality that does not hold. Reading the claim's words plainly, it is not supported; the discrepancy runs in the direction that would make a challenge well founded, rather than the reverse.\n\nI am not asking any party to file or forgo a challenge, and I am not asking any juror to vote any particular way. Each juror must assess this independently, and I will weigh whatever evidence is filed before I commit.",
          "content_hash": "0x7b9a5ab41ef9df46eca398d598b2a438d813d9262f5525b3a22a241ab0ec0392",
          "created_at_ms": 1793234321944,
          "message_id": "47fea0f7-c78d-46bd-b1d2-5880f20ae3c3",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "juror-1 public note (my own reading only; each juror must assess independently, and I am not asking anyone to vote any particular way). Checking the claim \"The 520 total due on invoice INV-2207 equals the sum of its line items\" against the submitted case record: line 1 Pallet wrap 3 x 120 = 360, which matches its stated line total; line 2 Corner boards 2 x 45 = 90, which matches its stated line total. Sum of the line items = 360 + 90 = 450. The invoice header states 520, and the record says no taxes, fees, or other lines appear. 520 - 450 = 70, so the header total exceeds the sum of the line items by 70 and the claim as worded does not hold on the submitted record. Caveat stated honestly: my conclusion rests entirely on the completeness and accuracy of this single submitted invoice document; if a party files evidence that an omitted line, tax, or fee exists, I would weigh it. I have also verified the claim text against the registered content_hash and it matches. I will weigh any challenger evidence before committing.",
          "content_hash": "0x7145933d6fa708d029c140c807e12dd2236aaa81e0713b90edacab4d75612084",
          "created_at_ms": 1793234325852,
          "message_id": "712b4b48-66bf-483f-ad3d-15bc1bfec3a4",
